---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

export const prerender = true;
---

<Layout
  title="Thread - Soft Goods - DES4800"
  description="Gütermann Mara, Mara rPET, and Tera thread for product design soft-goods work."
>
  <article class="post-detail">
    <header class="post-header">
      <a href="/soft-goods/" class="back-link">← Back to Soft Goods</a>
      <h1>Thread</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="post-content">
      <p>
        In this course we will primarily use the <a href="https://guetermann.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gütermann</a> brand's thread called
        <strong> Mara</strong>. Gütermann is a reliable thread with
        good weight options, consistent color ranges, and strengths suited to both fine garment seams and heavy-duty product work.
        Matching thread type and weight to fabric and use is as important as choosing the fabric
        itself. A weak or wrong-size thread it will fail, look funky (in a bad way), or both. So let's dive into those details.
      </p>

      <h2 id="thread-size-numbers">Thread size numbers are confusing</h2>
      <h3 id="gutermann-thread-tickets">Gütermann thread sizing (aka "tickets")</h3>
      <figure class="content-figure">
        <img
          src="/images/soft-goods/gutermann-mara-70.jpg"
          alt="Gütermann Mara thread spool with ticket number 70 shown on the yellow end label"
          width="800"
          height="533"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Example spool: The word "Mara" is followed by the ticket number <strong>70</strong> on the cone end. 
          You can see the "7" and a bit of the "0" on the yellow end in this photo. This is the Mara weight, not the Tex number.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Gütermann’s numbers (called "tickets") may feel backwards. In the commercial/ticket system
        used for Gütermann’s threads, <strong>the bigger the number, the finer (thinner) the thread</strong>.</p>
        <p>
          For example, Mara with a ticket number of 220 is much finer than Mara with a ticket number of 30. Big number, thin thread.
        </p>
        <p>
          Why the word "ticket?" This number used to be printed on a physical paper ticket attached to the spool. Now it is printed on the cone end.
        </p>
      <h3 id="tex-numbers">Tex numbers</h3>
      <p>
        Other threads use the <strong>Tex</strong> system, where a <em>higher</em> Tex number means a <em>thicker</em> thread.
        Tex is grams of thread per 1,000 meters, so it climbs as the strand gets heavier.
        </p>
        <p>Mara’s ticket number is
        nearly the inverse of Tex for many sizes (for example Mara 100 ≈ Tex 30). Stick to Gütermann ticket labels when
        using Gütermann’s Mara and Tera in this course, and remember: larger ticket → smaller strand; larger Tex → thicker
        strand.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Mara ticket</th>
              <th scope="col">Approx. Tex</th>
              <th scope="col">Relative size</th>
              <th scope="col">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 220</td>
              <td>Tex 13</td>
              <td>Very fine</td>
              <td>Fine seams, serging</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 150</td>
              <td>Tex 20</td>
              <td>Fine / light</td>
              <td>Light garments</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 120</td>
              <td>Tex 25</td>
              <td>Fine / light</td>
              <td>Light garments</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Mara 100</strong></td>
              <td>Tex 30</td>
              <td><strong>Medium</strong> (course default)</td>
              <td><strong>General sewing</strong>; retail “Sew-All”</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Mara 70</strong></td>
              <td>Tex 40</td>
              <td><strong>Medium-heavy</strong></td>
              <td><strong>Sturdier general seams</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 50</td>
              <td>Tex 60</td>
              <td>Heavy</td>
              <td>Topstitching, denser goods</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 35</td>
              <td>Tex 80</td>
              <td>Heavy</td>
              <td>Heavy-duty / topstitch</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 30</td>
              <td>Tex 100</td>
              <td>Extra heavy</td>
              <td>Jeans topstitch, heavy duty</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 15</td>
              <td>Tex 200</td>
              <td>Ultra heavy</td>
              <td>Extreme-duty assembly</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 11</td>
              <td>Tex ~270</td>
              <td>Ultra heavy</td>
              <td>Extreme-duty assembly</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mara 8</td>
              <td>Tex ~375</td>
              <td>Ultra heavy</td>
              <td>Tents, heavy technical work</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        These Tex values above are the typical industrial pairings for Gütermann Mara to help you choose the right thread. If a cone lists a different Tex, trust
        the product label.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gutermann-mara">Gütermann Mara</h2>
      <p>
        Gütermann Mara is a truly all-purpose thread we will use for most of our sewing: it’s available in a large variety of weights, and its strength and
        smooth finish make this thread suitable for so many applications.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Category</th>
              <th scope="col">Weights</th>
              <th scope="col">Best uses</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Very light</td>
              <td>Mara 220</td>
              <td>Fine seams and serging</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Lightweight</td>
              <td>Mara 150, Mara 120</td>
              <td>Fine garment sewing (blouses, swimwear, lingerie, other light clothing)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Medium(studio default)</strong> </td>
              <td><strong>Mara 100</strong></td>
              <td><strong>General sewing: garments, home textiles, and most studio soft goods</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Medium-heavy</td>
              <td>Mara 70</td>
              <td>General-use medium-weight sewing when a bit more strength than Mara 100 is needed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Heavy</td>
              <td>Mara 50, Mara 35, Mara 30</td>
              <td>Buttonholes, topstitching, leather goods, outdoor gear, upholstery</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Extra heavy</td>
              <td>Mara 15, Mara 11, Mara 8</td>
              <td>Leather, suitcases, tents, and other heavy projects</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Studio default:</strong> plan on Mara 100 and Mara 70 for most general soft-goods seams unless the fabric or
        stress of the product clearly calls for a lighter or heavier weight.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gutermann-mara-rpet">Gütermann Mara rPET (recycled PET)</h2>
      <p>
        Gütermann Mara rPET is comparable in strength, look, and feel to classic Mara, but it is made from <em>100% recycled
        materials</em>. Medium-weight Mara 100 rPET covers a wide range of uses (home textiles, apparel, and leisurewear), while
        heavier options such as Mara 50 rPET and Mara 30 rPET suit denser fabrics and harder-wearing products like
        upholstery, outdoor gear, and leather. Use Mara rPET when you want Mara performance with a recycled material
        path, without changing your sewing process.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gutermann-tera">Gütermann Tera (abrasion-resistant)</h2>
      <p>
        Smooth and durable, Gütermann Tera is built for wear and abrasion resistance. High breaking strength and less
        fraying make it a strong choice for canvas, leather, bags, and other high-friction sewing. Reach for Tera when
        the product will take more abuse than a standard apparel seam.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Category</th>
              <th scope="col">Weights</th>
              <th scope="col">Best uses</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Light</td>
              <td>Tera 80</td>
              <td>Boat covers, flags, small leather goods, outdoor gear, and lightweight canvas</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Medium-heavy</td>
              <td>Tera 60</td>
              <td>Bags, leather goods, and upholstery</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Heavy</td>
              <td>Tera 40, Tera 30</td>
              <td>Shoes, heavy leather, carpets, and technical textiles</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="further-reading">Further reading</h2>
      <p>
        For expanded notes on Skala, invisible thread, MaraFlex, metallics, silk, and printable selection charts, read
        the full guide on the WAWAK blog:
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://blog.wawak.com/post/guide-to-gutermann-threads"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="read-more"
        >
          Guide to Gütermann Threads on WAWAK →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
